dy-comments-removed","current_statement_sha256":"sha256:859ee5d7e13a4627fa0333415aef469d0865dc39825e24b72ab59732a1aac412","file":"preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","line":7,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"b6200b3038a5d00a69329ecf878830e98840ea0138a4e8f390a7a99a67836347","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:859ee5d7e13a4627fa0333415aef469d0865dc39825e24b72ab59732a1aac412","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex#L7"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:main}\nFix $s\\in(0,1]$ and $0<c<1/(4\\log2)$.  Along every sequence of instances $(n,q)$ with $n\\to\\infty$, $q\\in(0,1)$, and $q^3\\log n\\to\\infty$,\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:main-bound}\n T_{q,s}(n)\\ge n^{c\\log(q^3\\log n)}\n\\end{equation}\neventually.  All logarithms are natural.  In particular, for each fixed deletion probability $q\\in(0,1)$ and fixed positive success probability $s$, the required sample number is $n^{\\Omega(\\log\\log n)}$."}
